      "text": "Name: Racial-Sexual Hierarchy  \nType: Sociopolitical System  \nDescription: A legally enforced social structure that ranks individuals based on race and gender. Black men hold the highest status, followed by women of all races, while non-Black males are classified as Betas or \"whitebois\" and occupy the lowest tier. Challenging this hierarchy is considered deviant behavior and, in some jurisdictions, a punishable offense.  \nBackground: The hierarchy emerged as a result of historical power shifts and legal reforms aimed at redefining societal roles. Proponents argue it corrects past injustices, while critics decry it as institutionalized discrimination. Enforcement varies by region, with some areas applying strict legal penalties and others relying on social ostracism.  \nSpecial Traits:  \n- Legally recognized ranks with associated privileges or restrictions.  \n- Betas/whitebois may face employment, housing, or legal disadvantages.  \n- Women, while ranked second, still hold significant influence but are subordinate to Black men.  \n- Public dissent or defiance of the hierarchy can result in social or legal consequences.",
